use /lib
use /ui
from ../editor import EditorScope
from ../no-project import NoProjectStateScope
from ../open-file import OpenFileScope
from ../prompt-mode import PromptEditor
from ../prompt-mode/chat-input import ChatInputScope
from ../prompt-mode/message-list import MessageList
from /ui/components/file-tree import FileTreeScope
from /ui/components/inset-panel import InsetPanelScope
from /ui/components/project-sidebar import ProjectSidebarScope
from /ui/components/referenced-spec-sidebar import ReferencedSpecSidebarScope
from /ui/components/ribbon import RibbonScope

export ui-component BodyScope:
    concept: {BodyConcept}

concept BodyConcept:
    pitch:
        The main window's body: the area to the right of the project sidebar,
        where the tasks sent to the harness, and the files opened in tabs
        beside them, are shown, and the chat input they are sent from.

    shape: {BodyShape}

shape BodyShape:
    description:
        Beneath the @{RibbonScope}, to the right of the
        @{ProjectSidebarScope}, which runs the full height beside it, and
        reaching the window's right edge. It is part of the @{PromptEditor}.

        The @{ChatInputScope} lives within the body, along its bottom: its
        left edge against the project sidebar, and above it, beneath the
        body's tab bar, the selected tab's contents: the message list, or an
        open file. While a task runs, the @{ReferencedSpecSidebarScope} runs
        the body's full height at its right, beside the tab bar, the selected
        tab's contents, and the chat input, and the chat input's right edge is against it: the chat input
        never runs beneath the right sidebar. While that sidebar isn't there,
        the chat input reaches the window's right edge.

    contents:
        Everything that can be in the body:

        - a tab bar along its top, holding a Chat tab and a tab for each file open
        - the @{MessageList}, the chat history, shown beneath the tab bar while the Chat tab is selected
        - the hint shown in the message list's place before any task has been sent, or while no project is open, as the @{PromptEditor} and the @{NoProjectStateScope} say
        - an @{EditorScope} for each open file, shown beneath the tab bar while its tab is selected
        - the @{ChatInputScope}, along the bottom beneath whichever tab is selected
        - the @{ReferencedSpecSidebarScope}, at the right of all of these, the tab bar and the chat input included, while a task runs

        An @{InsetPanelScope} floats over the whole window, the body included,
        but is never part of it.

    tabs:
        Along the top of the body runs a tab bar, in gpui-kit's default tab
        styling, across the width the message list takes: from the project
        sidebar to the referenced spec sidebar while a task runs, and to the
        window's right edge otherwise. Its first tab, Chat, shows the message
        list, and is always there: it can't be closed or moved. After it comes
        a tab for each open file, in the order they were opened, scrolling
        sideways once there are more than fit.

        Just one tab's contents show at a time, filling the space between the
        tab bar and the chat input. The chat input stays along the bottom
        whichever tab is selected, so a prompt can be written while a file
        shows. The body starts with Chat selected, and clicking a tab selects
        it.

        A file's tab shows the file's name, then, while it has unsaved
        changes, a dot, and at its right a small close button. Hovering it
        shows the file's path in the project as its tooltip.

    openFile:
        A file opened, from the @{FileTreeScope} or anywhere else the
        @{OpenFileScope} says, shows in an @{EditorScope} in a tab of its own,
        as many files open at once as are opened. A file not open yet gets a
        new tab, just after the one selected, and is selected. Opening a file
        that already has a tab selects that tab instead, leaving its editor
        as it was, unsaved changes and all.

        Nothing slides: a file's tab is selected, and its editor shows, at
        once, and closing it takes it away at once too. There is no split,
        and no edge to drag; the file takes the tab bar's full width above the
        chat input.

        Closing a file's tab, with its close button or the editor's, closes
        the file, the editor first asking to discard any unsaved changes.
        Closing the selected tab selects the tab to its right, or, with none
        to its right, the one to its left, which is Chat once no file is
        left. Closing a tab that isn't selected leaves the selection as it
        is.
